      "title": "Accounts Payable Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-26\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nWestminster University Job Title: Accounts Payable Lead Accountant Reports To: Assistant Controller Department: Finance & Accounting Accounts Payable Lead Accountant The Accounts Payable Lead Accountant is responsible for leading the daily accounts payable operations for Westminster University of Utah. This position oversees invoice processing, payment disbursements, vendor maintenance, employee reimbursements, purchase-order matching, accruals, reconciliations, and related accounting activities. The role supports accurate financial reporting, strong internal controls, timely payments, and responsive service to campus departments and external vendors. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Lead daily accounts payable operations, including invoice review, payment processing, employee reimbursements, vendor maintenance, and AP processing queues. Review transactions for accuracy, completeness, documentation, approval, and proper fund, department, account, and general ledger coding. Support and guide accounts payable staff, including assigning work, reviewing output, answering questions, and helping resolve processing issues. Serve as the primary escalation point for vendor inquiries, payment status questions, campus department support, and invoice or purchasing issues. Partner with Procurement, Finance colleagues, and campus departments to resolve purchase-order matching exceptions and ensure compliance with University payment processes. Prepare AP-related journal entries, accruals, prepaid expense schedules, account reconciliations, and supporting documentation for month-end and year-end close. Reconcile the accounts payable subledger to the general ledger and research or resolve outstanding balances, aging items, and discrepancies. Lead weekly check, ACH, and wire payment runs in accordance with University policies, cash management needs, and vendor payment terms. Maintain vendor records and support compliance with W-9, 1099, tax reporting, and internal documentation requirements. Support audit preparation by maintaining complete records, responding to audit requests, and ensuring transactions are properly supported. Help strengthen internal controls to prevent duplicate payments, unauthorized disbursements, fraud risk, and processing errors. Assist campus departments with expense questions, budget-to-actual review, fund restrictions, and appropriate use of University resources. Identify opportunities to streamline AP workflows, improve electronic invoice intake, strengthen reimbursement and corporate card processes, and reduce manual work. Prepare reports, schedules, and analyses related to payable activity, vendor payments, accruals, and University expense activity.   Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or a related field required. Minimum of four years of full-cycle accounts payable, accounting operations, or related finance experience required. At least one to two years of experience in a senior, lead, or supervisory role preferred. Strong understanding of accounts payable processes, accruals, account reconciliations, internal controls, and general ledger coding. Knowledge of W-9, 1099, vendor maintenance, payment processing, and supporting documentation requirements. Strong customer-service orientation and ability to communicate clearly and professionally with campus departments, vendors, and Finance colleagues. Excellent attention to detail, organizational skills, and ability to manage multiple deadlines in a high-volume environment. Proficiency with Microsoft Excel and financial systems required. Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, nonprofit, or fund-accounting environments. Experience with Ellucian Colleague or similar ERP systems. Experience supporting fiscal year-end close, audit preparation, prepaid expenses, accruals, and financial reporting schedules. Familiarity with purchase-order workflows, procurement processes, corporate card administration, and employee reimbursement programs. Advanced Excel skills, including pivot tables, XLOOKUP, account reconciliations, and financial analysis.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201264838532_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Nursing (Graduate Fami",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-17\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nAssistant Professor of Nursing (Graduate Family Nurse Practitioner Program)  The Graduate Family Nurse Practitioner Program within the School of Nursing and Health Sciences at Westminster University in Salt Lake City, Utah invites applications for a full-time faculty position. ($82,000to $84,000(11 month Contract) We seek candidates with expertise in a variety of nursing curricula such as but not limited to; Advanced Health Assessment, Health Care and Promotion of the Adult, Older Adult, Young Adult, and Infants through Adolescents, and Ambulatory Procedures. Applicants must have experience in teaching graduate nursing students using the New Essentials Core Competencies (AACN 2021) in all aspects of graduate education, which include nursing courses in the didactic setting, clinical student teaching, and supervision in skills lab, simulation, and healthcare and community settings.  The teaching load for this full-time position is 32-semester hours that include fall, spring, and summer semesters. A standard contract at Westminster University is a 9-month contract. The range for a 9-month contract would be $70,772-$74,704. The contract for this position is an 11-months beginning in August 2026 with the adjusted 11-month salary above.  Westminster University’s commitment to diversity and global learning is revealed in its core values, mission and vision statements, and learning goals. We seek colleagues who will support the learning of all students, including those from groups historically underrepresented in higher education, such as students of color, LGBTQ+ students, students with disabilities, and first-generation college students. We invite candidates whose interests and skills contribute to these efforts. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: A PhD in nursing from a regionally accredited institution is preferred. A Doctorate in Nursing Practice (DNP), Master of Science in Nursing (MSN), or Master of Science in Nursing Education (MSNEd) from a regionally accredited institution may be considered.  In addition, the nursing school where the nursing degree was obtained must be accredited through CCNE or ACEN. We seek candidates with an excellent teaching record and ability to engage with students to enhance learning. Westminster University’s commitment to diversity and global learning is revealed in its core values, mission and vision statements, and institutional priorities.  PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS:  The ideal candidate will have a record of graduate teaching experience in an academic setting and be able to demonstrate skills, experience, and potential to facilitate the learning of all students, including those historically underrepresented in higher education such as students of color, LGBTQIA students, students with disabilities, first-generation college students, and more. In addition, the candidate will have expertise in the use of technology in the classroom, online, and other instruction as identified.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201263841280_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Senior Accountant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-14\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nWestminster University Senior Accountant Position Type: Full-Time, Benefited Work Location: Westminster University Campus 1840 South 1300 East Salt Lake City, UT 84105 About Westminster University: Westminster University is a private, independent, and comprehensive university in Salt Lake City, Utah. For more than 150 years, Westminster has combined academic excellence with a strong sense of community and purpose. Located at the base of the Wasatch Mountains, the university offers the energy of a growing metropolitan area alongside world-class outdoor recreation just minutes from campus. Our campus is collaborative, student-centered, and grounded in academic integrity. Faculty and staff work closely with students to foster critical thinking, career readiness, and meaningful engagement beyond the classroom. Employees are supported in their professional growth and encouraged to bring forward ideas that strengthen the university. Westminster is committed to creating a workplace where people thrive – offering flexibility, generous time off, and a culture that values balance, inclusion, and innovation. Position Summary: The Senior Accountant acts as a member of the finance team, which holds ownership over the general ledger, accounts payable, accounts receivable (student accounts), payroll, financial reporting, departmental and entity-wide budget process, internal controls, and compliance. The Senior Accountant will oversee processes and reporting for grants and internal accounting functions related to select general ledger funds, accounts payable, and University assets. Position Responsibilities: Grant and Restricted Funds Accounting and Compliance The incumbent will provide comprehensive accounting and financial services across the full lifecycle of sponsored awards, from pre-award budget development through to final financial reporting and audit closeout. Working in close collaboration with academic departments, the Development office, and external funding bodies, the Grants and Contracts Accountant will ensure that all financial activity is conducted in accordance with Federal Uniform Guidance (2 CFR Part 200), sponsor terms and conditions, and Westminster University's financial policies and procedures. Provide professional-level accounting, budgetary, and financial support for federal, state, local, and private grants and contracts awarded to the University. Ensure fiscal compliance with all internal and external rules and regulations. Prepare and approve all invoices and financial reports in accordance with grant/contract requirements. Provide financial oversight and guidance for various academic and business operations, activities, and program accounts. Assist principal investigators with grant principles, grant-specific budgeting, and cost allowance issues. Assist with difficult purchasing decisions. Perform risk analysis and monitor compliance with financial related policies, procedures, laws, regulations, and established internal controls. Work closely with grantor entities and with auditors to resolve queries and assist in resolving audit findings. Work closely with the Director of Sponsored Projects and Associate Provost for Research and Creative Activities regarding the completion of requested pre-award and all post-award grant and contract financial related tasks and issues. Maintain regular meetings with grant principles to review current data and reporting requirements. Perform monthly/quarterly processes for indirect cost recording and time and effort reporting. Prepare journal entries, reallocation of transactions between accounts, and process billings. Monitor, provide support, and reconcile restricted fund accounting to ensure funds are used in accordance with donor intent. Fixed Assets Oversee all fixed assets including the library and fine art collections. Maintain the asset database and other schedules with additions, disposals, and depreciation transactions. Prepare monthly journal entries and annual reporting for the audit and other reporting. Coordinate with departments across campus to ensure compliance with the capitalization policy and disposal of assets. Other Financial Duties Monitor interdepartmental transactions and internal organizations to ensure proper accounting and financial reporting. Review of expenses and check registers to ensure proper accounting classification and controls, as well as identify payments to be reclassified as assets. Assist with gathering and reporting data for the annual audit and tax documents. Prepare reporting for internal activity with the Spirit Store and dining service. Review and assist with activity in the agency funds, including Associated Students of Westminster and the Westminster Woman’s Board accounts. Oversee the stale dated check process as the contact with the State of Utah. Review and adjust restricted fund accounts. Diversity and Inclusion Promote an inclusive Westminster through demonstration of the commitments include in the Westminster University Diversity Statement. Participate in developmental activities to increase understanding and awareness of issues related diversity, equity and inclusion both individually and as related to our filed of work, profession or discipline. Minimum Qualifications Bachelor’s degree required and Master’s degree preferred, and related accounting experience and/or training. Minimum of four years related experience. Advanced Excel spreadsheet and database skills. High level of analytical and critical thinking skills, problem-solving abilities. Ability to handle multiple priorities and meet deadlines. What we offer: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance  Paid Time Off (PTO) Paid Parental Leave Paid Family and Medical Leave 403(b) Retirement Plan with a 5.5% employer contribution Tuition Remission (after a qualifying period of employment) 18 Paid Holidays Annually Cell Phone Plan Discounts Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201261745470_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Design Lead",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-11\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nDesign Lead Westminster University Supervisor: Director of Marketing Department: Office of Marketing, Communication, and Events Position Summary The Design Lead is Westminster's visual brand steward, reporting to the Director of Marketing. This role moves beyond brand maintenance into proactively shaping where Westminster's brand goes next, bringing initiative, strategic vision, and hands-on craft to every project. Reporting to the Director of Marketing and in partnership with the Junior Graphic Designer, the Design Lead originates creative concepts and campaign thinking, translates institutional strategy into compelling visual solutions, and actively problem-solves to elevate the brand across print, digital, social, advertising, and editorial materials. They work cross-functionally with the internal MarComm team and campus partners. The ideal candidate pairs strong design execution with strategic, big-picture thinking: someone who is constantly asking how Westminster's brand can be stronger, more cohesive, and more distinctive. They're excited to bring that thinking to a mission-driven institution with a strong sense of community and a real commitment to educational values, and they're energized by the cultural and creative life around them. Essential Duties and Responsibilities CREATIVE DIRECTION AND BRAND Serve as vision holder for Westminster's brand, proactively identifying opportunities to strengthen and evolve the university's visual identity, and contributing creative concepts and strategic design solutions that advance university goals and elevate projects from concept through execution Develop wireframes and visual direction for campaign initiatives, partnering with the web team and other campus partners Evaluate creative work across departments to identify where visual direction should shift, translating institutional priorities into cohesive visual communications Ensure consistency, quality, and brand alignment across all marketing and communications materials, print through digital   BRAND OPERATIONS AND PROJECT MANAGEMENT  Participate in project intake with the Director of Marketing, bringing design-specific questions to define project goals and creative briefs Manage individual design projects and deadlines to ensure high-quality, timely execution Strategize, design, and produce the Westminster Review magazine, annual event invitations, and other institutional publications Translate campaigns across mediums — platform specs, reformatting, ensuring one campaign holds together from print to web Develop and maintain university-wide design templates (Canva, PowerPoint, and other branded resources) Co-manage print production and vendor relationships (estimates, specs, timelines, delivery); support sourcing of freelance creatives and specialty vendors Maintain Westminster's brand portal (CLC Brand Manager 360) Present creative work and brand direction to stakeholders, advocating for strategic design Solve production and creative challenges proactively Stay current on design trends and production best practices TEAM LEADERSHIP AND MENTORSHIP Mentor the Junior Graphic Designer and student employees, fostering craft and creative growth; track the team's project pipeline with the Director of Marketing Present creative work and brand direction to external stakeholders/clients and internal teams Partner closely with the Director of Marketing as a strategic creative counterpart — helping shape direction, not just receiving it Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Graphic Design, Visual Communications, or related field 7+ years of professional graphic design experience, including meaningful agency or client-facing time and demonstrated growth into higher-level creative ownership Strong portfolio demonstrating brand identity and systems-level thinking — not just campaign execution — across print, digital, social, web, and editorial Demonstrated experience developing wireframes or UX/interface design for websites or digital products Familiarity with Canva and PowerPoint, to support campus clients effectively Demonstrated experience presenting creative work to executive stakeholders and/or external clients, and navigating feedback with confidence Proven ability to translate a campaign or brand across multiple channels while maintaining cohesion Expert proficiency in Adobe Creative Suite and Figma; working proficiency in XD or similar Exceptional written, verbal, and visual communication skills Preferred Qualifications Motion graphics or animation experience (After Effects, Premiere Pro, or similar) Illustration skills, or experience writing briefs for and managing illustrators/specialty creatives Experience in higher education or another complex, multi-stakeholder institutional environment Mentorship experience Editorial design experience for print publications (magazines, annual reports, or similar) Familiarity with brand portal/DAM systems (e.g., CLC Brand Manager 360) or similar platforms Diversity and Inclusion: Promote an Inclusive Westminster through demonstration of the commitments included in the Westminster University Diversity Statement. Participate in developmental activities to increase understanding and awareness of issues related diversity, equity and Inclusion both individually and as related to my field of work, profession or discipline. Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201252476292_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Associate Director,  Residence Life",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-22\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nAssociate Director, Residence Life Westminster University   Position Type:  Full-Time, Benefited Work Location:  Westminster University Campus 1840 South 1300 East Salt Lake City, UT 84105     What we offer: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance  Paid Time Off (PTO) Paid Parental Leave Paid Family and Medical Leave 403(b) Retirement Plan with a 5.5% employer contribution Tuition Remission (after a qualifying period of employment) 18 Paid Holidays Annually Cell Phone Plan Discounts Please apply directly through Westminster University's Career Job Posting .   A completed application will include:   Resume  Cover letter that describes how your previous experience will contribute to this role and the Westminster community.  Three professional references with their full name, job title, organization name, relationship to the applicant, and contact information. Please submit all required documents through the single Dropbox provided during your application.   About the Role: As an integral member of the Division of Student Affairs, the Associate Director reports to the Director of Residence Life and Student Dining and provides leadership in the development and administration of a comprehensive, preeminent residence life program. This position advances a high-impact residential experience for approximately 600 students across five residence hall communities. The Associate Director has primary responsibility for departmental operations, including housing occupancy management, facilities management, risk management, coordination of summer camps and conferences, and the implementation of short- and long-term departmental projects. The position also cultivates and maintains collaborative partnerships with key university departments, including Athletics, Facilities, Campus Safety and Security, the Dean of Students Office, Student Accounts, Student Engagement and Belonging, and Disability Services. The Associate Director participates in a rotating on-call schedule and serves as a primary responder to student concerns and crises, reported incidents, alleged policy violations, and emergencies affecting the residential community. This is a 12-month, full-time, benefited position. The Associate Director is required to reside on campus and will be provided a semi-furnished, non-taxable residence, including utilities, internet, cable, and a parking pass, as part of the compensation package and in direct support of the operational responsibilities of the position. Student Affairs at Westminster is committed to creating an environment that encourages students' exploration of self while pursuing community and working toward academic success. Westminster is a private comprehensive liberal arts institution with a deep commitment preparing students to lead lives of learning, accomplishment, and service and to help them develop skills and attributes vital for success in a diverse and interdependent world. Westminster centralizes engaged learning that emphasizes theory and practice academic, co-curricular and extra-curricular programs. A culture of imagination and innovation with a committed practice of inclusiveness and respect for differences. Westminster University is in the trendy walkable Sugar House neighborhood with easy access to the Rocky Mountains, downtown Salt Lake City, and many of Utah's natural wonders. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree from an accredited institution in College Student Personnel, Higher Education Administration or related field.  Minimum of two years of professional experience in university housing, residence life, conference services or related student affairs work.  Working knowledge of FERPA, Clery, Title IX, ADA and Section 504, and related higher education laws.  Demonstrated experience working with college students.  Demonstrated experience supervising professional staff and leading teams. Demonstrated knowledge and commitment to advancing diverse, equitable, inclusive, and accessible communities.  Leadership competencies and skills in problem-solving, responding to crises and emergencies of varying severity, and operational execution.  Preferred Qualifications: Experience working with on-campus living, residential populations.  Experience with case management, housing management, or student information systems such as Personna, eRezLife, Star Rez, Ellucian Colleague, Advocate or Maxient.    Responsibilities: Principle Duties and Responsibilities Education, Engagement & Student Success: Promote student learning, leadership development, and success through intentional programming and community-building efforts. Serve as a departmental Conduct Officer vested with authority to investigate and adjudicate allegations of non-academic misconduct consistent with procedures outlined in the Code of Conduct and as directed by the Assistant Dean of Students of Student Life and Operations.  Facilitate various alternative dispute and conflict resolution processes (e.g., mediation) in lieu of formal adjudication.  Collaborate with key academic affairs and student affairs partners to enhance student learning within residential communities. Ensure proper storage of education records, documentation and administrative case management in Maxient student conduct database in compliance with FERPA, student privacy protections, and university policy.  Provide guidance and support to staff regarding student concerns, behavioral issues, and community dynamics. Housing Operations and Administrative Management: Work with professional and student staff to create and maintain a living and learning environment that supports the mission of Westminster University and Residence Life. Provide leadership in ongoing evaluation of services and program operations related to residence hall management and the residential student experience. Responsible for preparing and maintaining operations timelines, such as reservations, assignments, communications, and move in and move out. Develops and coordinates the room transfer process, winter break and summer term assignments, and the annual housing selection process, and waiting list processes. Provide leadership and management for housing assignments including supervising room management workflow of Residence Life coordinators. Monitor and report room occupancies, and other housing related operations; ensure rosters and occupancy reports are updated and current reflecting room assignment changes. Provide monthly and annual occupancy reports to the Director and appropriate departments and campus partners. Coordinate yearly assignment configuration planning, maintain configuration rosters and floor plans to maximize occupancy, and report plans on maximizing occupancy to the Director.  Manage and maximize residence hall facility management software.  In conjunction with Student Accounts, manage room billing and housing related charges.  Lead and coordinate move-in and move-out processes including residence hall openings and closings. Manage residential mail and package collection and distribution processes. Facilities, Occupancy and Residential Services: Coordinate with Facilities to minimize the impact of emergency and planned maintenance and facilities work on residents.  Collaborate closely with Facilities to report residence hall maintenance and custodial issues and monitor progress toward completion.  Oversee coordinated security of residence halls in partnership with Campus Security. Coordinate with Campus Safety to schedule and facilitate fire drills and fire alarm inspections/testing within the residence halls Work in conjunction with Disability Student Services on resident student accommodations.  Provide departmental leadership for identifying and assigning accessible living spaces. Coordinate and manage the distribution and tracking of all residential keys and equipment, including card access and key replacements to residence hall facilities. Coordinate with Facilities to establish the annual calendar of residence hall cleaning, maintenance, and inventory replacement. Collaborate closely with Facilities on residence hall furniture inventory, storage, and replacement. Market residence hall facilities with creativity and innovative methods. Team Development and Supervision: Directly supervise office student staff responsible for customer service regarding housing assignments and facilities, receiving emergency and crisis assistance requests from residents before passing to professional staff, and working on administrative projects. Provides leadership and management of front desk services, including email and phone customer services; recurring check-in and checkout processes, including residence hall openings and closures, including but not limited to early arrivals, late stays, failed departures, etc. Assist professional staff with student employee hiring, training, scheduling, and accountability. Supervise and manage Community Leaders in the functional areas of residence hall facilities. Manage student employment administrative processes. Coordinate and/or deliver staff training related to technology, facilities, assignments, customer service, and overall housing operations. Develop Community Leader training and ongoing education specific to institutional policy, standard operating procedures, protocols, and emergency response procedures to ensure Community Leaders are appropriately confronting and documenting behavioral issues and concerns. Assist in the identification and delivery of departmental goal setting and performance evaluations for facilities and student staff. Summer Conferences and External Housing Programs: Coordinate and manage residential summer conferences program, including staff selection and training, logistics, emergency response. Liaise with Campus Dining, Event Services, Campus Security, Facilities and other partners to coordinate all aspects of Summer Camps and Conferences. Manage residential summer camp inquiries, sales, invoicing, and budget management. Develop and maintain relationships and serve as a primary site contact for conference groups. Coordinate housing arrangements for guests requiring on-campus housing during their camp or conference. Leadership, Assessment and University Service: Assist with departmental policy and procedure management; identify and develop needed departmental policies and procedures. Assist Department Director with annual strategic planning and assessment. Assist with department budget management, including processing purchases and tracking spending. Meet regularly with Department Director to review operations. Identify issues of area or departmental concern and take initiative to resolve or report them to director and assist in the identification of potential solutions. Promote and maintain an atmosphere of excellent internal and external customer service within Residence Life. Serve on divisional and other University committees/work groups as assigned. Attend professional workshops, conferences, campus events, etc. Perform other duties as assigned.       Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201245931991_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director, Foundation Relations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-09\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nDirector of Foundation Relations Westminster University   Position Type:  Full-Time, Benefited Work Location:  Westminster University Campus 1840 South 1300 East Salt Lake City, UT 84105     What we offer:   Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance  Paid Time Off (PTO) Paid Parental Leave Paid Family and Medical Leave 403(b) Retirement Plan with a 5.5% employer contribution Tuition Remission (after a qualifying period of employment) 18 Paid Holidays Annually Cell Phone Plan Discounts Please apply directly through   Westminster University's Career Job Posting .   A completed application will include: Resume  Cover letter that describes how your previous experience will contribute to this role and the Westminster community.  Three professional references with their full name, job title, organization name, relationship to the applicant, and contact information. Please submit all required documents through the single Dropbox provided during your application.   About the Role: The Director of Foundation Relations is a frontline fundraiser responsible for securing institutional grants from private and family foundations and may, at times, support and collaborate to secure government, agency, and corporate opportunities as well. The director plans and executes the full grant lifecycle for the existing foundation portfolio at Westminster University, collaborates with institutional leadership to align solicitations with university priorities, and handles the identification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of new national and regional foundation opportunities. Occasionally, the director will engage and solicit individuals as assigned in coordination with the prospect research office. The director develops strategy and planning milestones for foundations during Westminster’s current comprehensive fundraising campaign and other university or foundation-prioritized initiatives. This often involves a range of cultivation to proposal submission conversations that can impact lead gift generation and establish agreements related to secured funding. The director initiates and conducts personal visits with key foundation contacts as needed and accompanies university leadership and campus partners (faculty and staff) on foundation visits as necessary. Responsibilities also include the oversight and execution of all stages of proposal development with campus partners, grant writing, grant reporting, and foundation application processes. The director provides timely reporting to external and internal stakeholders and conducts routine financial tracking of grants and potential grants in Westminster’s Institutional Advancement CRM, Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge/NXT. The ability to manage a variety of significant university relationships within a moves management process, function independently, and balance multiple deadlines with competing priorities is essential. Exceptional organizational skills and attention to detail are a must. A genuine enjoyment of working with a diverse constituent base, including campus partners, alumni, donors, and friends, will ensure this position’s success. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s Degree required 5 years + of progressively responsible experience in institutional advancement, foundation  relations, grant writing, or other, related, nonprofit development Strong interpersonal skills and ability to initiate communication with members  of Westminster University’s community Excellent written and verbal communication skills   Proven ability to work in a team environment   Strong organizational skills and attention to detail   Excellent multitasking and time management skills; ability to be self-motivated to make   progress on projects and goals, and to be accountable Professionalism, maturity, and an ability to maintain confidentiality   Preferred Qualifications: Master’s Degree  2 years + of direct experience cultivating foundation prospects and securing funding from institutional or private foundations Proven track record in successfully managing a portfolio of prospects and closing complex philanthropic gifts or grants at the six and seven figure level Responsibilities: Reporting to the Vice President of Institutional Advancement, the Director of Foundation Relations will: Proposal & Grant Management: Oversee all stages of proposal development, including drafting Letters of Inquiry (LOIs), comprehensive grant proposals, and case statements, while ensuring they adhere to strict formatting and budget requirements. Relationship Building & Solicitation: Maintain a portfolio of active foundation prospects, which includes coordinating site visits and facilitating meetings between foundation officers and university leadership, board members, and campus partners. Stewardship & Compliance: Oversee post-award reporting and compliance, ensuring grant funds are used as directed. This includes updating foundation contacts on the impact of their gift commitments to encourage future funding. Strategy & Pipeline Management: Develop annual strategic plans to secure annual and major gifts from foundation opportunities. Continuously research and identify prospective funders whose missions align with Westminster’s priorities and programs.  Cross-Functional Collaboration: Act as a liaison between Institutional Advancement (IA) and internal offices as necessary to ensure compelling proposal development and execution of grants & gift commitments, which may include an assigned liaison role as IA’s centralized point of contact to a campus Dean and School/College and their advisory board of volunteers for funding and engagement priorities. Portfolio Growth: Identify, cultivate, solicit, and steward new national and regional foundation opportunities; engage and solicit individuals, as assigned, in coordination with the prospect research office.  Campaign Strategy: Develop foundation relationship strategies and planning milestones for Westminster’s comprehensive fundraising campaign and other prioritized initiatives to ensure a positive trajectory. Systems Management: Responsibly document all work with prospects and funders into the constituent relationship management system (Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge/NXT) in a timely manner, in accordance with Institutional Advancement policies and procedures. Additional Duties: Perform other duties as assigned.     Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201242067763_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Office Manager, Integrated Wellness",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-30\n\nOffice Manager, Integrated Wellness Westminster University Position Type: Full-Time, Benefited, 10-Month Position Year Schedule:  August 1st through May 31st. Work Location: Westminster University Campus 1840 South 1300 East Salt Lake City, UT 84105 What we offer: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance  Paid Time Off (PTO) Paid Parental Leave Paid Family and Medical Leave 403(b) Retirement Plan with a 5.5% employer contribution Tuition Remission (after a qualifying period of employment) 18 Paid Holidays Annually Cell Phone Plan Discounts Please apply directly through Westminster University's Job Posting A completed application will include: Resume  Cover letter that describes how your previous experience will contribute to this role and the Westminster community.  Three professional references with their full name, job title, organization name, relationship to the applicant, and contact information. Please submit all required documents through the single Dropbox provided during your application. Applications received by July 19, 2026 , will receive first consideration. Applications submitted after this date may be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. About Westminster University: Westminster University is a private, independent, and comprehensive university in Salt Lake City, Utah. For more than 150 years, Westminster has combined academic excellence with a strong sense of community and purpose. Located at the base of the Wasatch Mountains, the university offers the energy of a growing metropolitan area alongside world-class outdoor recreation just minutes from campus. Our campus is collaborative, student-centered, and grounded in academic integrity. Faculty and staff work closely with students to foster critical thinking, career readiness, and meaningful engagement beyond the classroom. Employees are supported in their professional growth and encouraged to bring forward ideas that strengthen the university. Westminster is committed to creating a workplace where people thrive – offering flexibility, generous time off, and a culture that values balance, inclusion, and innovation. About the Role: Student Affairs at Westminster University is committed to fostering an equitable, inclusive, safe, and well campus community. The division supports students by cultivating a vibrant campus life rooted in radical inclusivity, diversity, engagement, and belonging. Student Affairs approaches student challenge and support through a holistic framework that centers personal identity development, values clarification, commitment to wellness, and purpose-driven living. Integrated Wellness is a department within Student Affairs that provides comprehensive, coordinated health and wellness services to the student community. The department includes a Director, Office Manager as outlined in this job description, Nurse Practitioner(s), Mental Health Therapist(s), and clinical trainee(s). Reporting directly to the Director of Integrated Wellness, the Office Manager for Integrated Wellness provides administrative and clerical oversight of Integrated Wellness Medical Services and Mental Health Services as well as the L.S. Skaggs Integrated Wellness Center. The position oversees the daily operations of the front lobby and coordinates workflow and projects to ensure the successful administration of Integrated Wellness initiatives.  This is a full-time, non-exempt position scheduled at 40 hours per week for 10 months annually (.83 FTE) from August 1st through May 31st (June and July off). The role is ideal for an administrative professional seeking a consistent schedule with extended planned time off during the year. The successful candidate will be one who enthusiastically desires to serve undergraduate and graduate students and the broader campus community. Occasional evening or weekend hours may be required to support university events and peak service periods.  Essential Duties and Responsibilities: Provide coverage and administrative support in the L.S. Skaggs Integrated Wellness Center lobby. Serve as a welcoming first point of contact for students accessing medical and/ or mental health services and others who interface with Integrated Wellness at Westminster. Maintain and manage clinic appointment scheduling and calendars for providers. Support Director of Integrated Wellness with booking meetings and appointments as needed. Answer phones and respond to emails and requests for service professionally and confidentially. Maintain electronic versions of medical and mental health forms in both Microsoft Word and the electronic health record (EHR), interfacing with the EHR vendor as needed. Maintain strict compliance with relevant privacy regulations including FERPA and HIPAA. Coordinate, design, schedule, and produce marketing assets and copy for Integrated Wellness initiatives including Canva flyers, Instagram posts, communications with campus, and coordination with Westminster Marketing Communications as needed. Oversee operation of the L.S. Skaggs Integrated Wellness Center building including: review and approval of room reservation requests; train campus colleagues on use of Test Kitchen and ensure adherence to expectedprocedures;maintainkitchensuppliesandlaundertowels;coordinatewithCampusEventsas needed; and log and manage building maintenance requests. Assist Medical Services with patient payment processing and room changeover/ cleaning.  Maintain the department’s referral list of community providers. Attend weekly staff meetings, manage the agenda and meeting notes, and track and assist with follow-through of assigned tasks. Manage wellness-related operations including supply ordering, meeting scheduling, facility reservation and preparation, data entry and compilation, and creation of reports and presentations. Provide financial assistance to Director including: process student medical payments in EHR and campus system; track purchases and receipts; maintain accurate financial records; process check requests and obtain signatures; purchasing card approval; maintain internal budget trackers; monthly review and reconciliation of departmental budget allocations; and communicate regularly with the Accounting Office for questions, errors, or discrepancies. Liaise with contractors to maintain office supplies, deliveries, and building maintenance. In alignment with Integrated Wellness initiatives, cultivate collaborative partnerships with student groups and campus departments (e.g., Residence Life, Athletics, Academic Support, DEI, Academic Affairs, etc.) to embed wellness into the student experience. Additional Responsibilities: Participate in the collaborative planning and staffing of university-wide events including new student orientation, convocation, commencement, and tabling events. Assist with divisional needs as assigned during low-demand times in Integrated Wellness, especially over student breaks. Promote an Inclusive Westminster through demonstration of the commitments included in the Westminster Diversity Statement. Participate in developmental activities to increase understanding and awareness of issues related to diversity, equity, and inclusion both individually and as related to my field of work, profession, or discipline.  Other duties as assigned  Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree and two years related experience Excellent written and verbal communication skills Strong customer service orientation both internally and externally with excellent phone etiquette and interpersonal skills Excellent computer skills required including accurate typing skill of 50+ wpm, proficiency in Microsoft Office Suite, specifically word processing, spreadsheets, presentation and database software, internet applications and social media Excellent administrative skills, including an ability to collaborate with campus community and programs Strong organizational skills, able to work independently, multi-task, and manage project timelines Attention to detail and problem-solving skills Experience with diverse populations and a global orientation Positive professional demeanor that instills confidence, trust and respect Ability to maintain confidentiality in all interactions Comfort with and desire to work in a student-facing environment with shifting priorities and traffic Willingness to learn and use university databases/ software systems as needed Commitment to teamwork and working across teams when necessary Commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion to affirm students’ intersecting identities as they access wellness services at Westminster Preferred Qualifications: Three years of progressively responsible administrative experience in higher education Expertise and previous work experience in a university medical, mental health, or integrated wellness clinic Experience with triage or intake processes Familiarity with Medicator other EHRs Bilingual or multilingual  Physical Requirements: Must be able to sit, stand, see, hear, lift, and bend; Occasional outdoor work is required. Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=8b104a38-45ac-4682-9e01-117585322eb0&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201214421257_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director, Institutional Communications",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-06\n\nLocation: Salt Lake City\n\nWestminster University Director of Institutional Communications Position Type: Full-Time, Benefited Work Location: Westminster University Campus 1840 South 1300 East Salt Lake City, UT 84105 Application Instructions: Please apply directly through Westminster University's Career Job Posting . A completed application will include a resume and cover lette r that describes how your previous experience will contribute to this role and the Westminster community. Please submit all required documents through the single Dropbox provided . About Westminster University: Westminster University is a private, independent, and comprehensive university in Salt Lake City, Utah. For more than 150 years, Westminster has combined academic excellence with a strong sense of community and purpose. Located at the base of the Wasatch Mountains, the university offers the energy of a growing metropolitan area alongside world-class outdoor recreation just minutes from campus. Our campus is collaborative, student-centered, and grounded in academic integrity. Faculty and staff work closely with students to foster critical thinking, career readiness, and meaningful engagement beyond the classroom. Employees are supported in their professional growth and encouraged to bring forward ideas that strengthen the university. Westminster is committed to creating a workplace where people thrive – offering flexibility, generous time off, and a culture that values balance, inclusion, and innovation. About the Role: Reporting to the Chief Communications Officer, the Director of Institutional Communications drives strategies for and implementation of a range of external communications and marketing projects. Working to build the university’s reputation and brand expression, this role leads communications and marketing strategy for the university’s programs and initiatives and supports student success through communicating the student experience to Westminster audiences and the public at large. This position leads a team of digital media and marketing professionals. Minimum Qualifications: Four (4)years or more of work experience in communications, public relations, marketing, or other closely-related fields. Bachelor’s degree or higher in communications, marketing, or a related field. Demonstrable experience with principles and concepts in public relations and media relations, and familiarity with marketing, social media, copywriting, photography, graphic design, audio and video production. Preferred Qualifications: One (1) year or more of managing individuals who report to you. Additional Requirements: May be designated as essential personnel in cases of campus closure or emergency. Extended hours, including evenings and weekends will be required. Ability to respond on-call as assigned, including evenings and weekends is required. Responsibilities: Communications Develop, manage, and execute a strategic communications strategy that elevates the visibility of the university and its leadership. Serve as the point of contact for media outlets and reporters. Field all incoming media requests and serve as the university’s spokesperson. Develop messaging and narrative structures for media campaigns and materials such as press releases, media advisories, and pitches. Responsible for news coverage monitoring, analysis, and internal visibility to continuously elevate and adjust communications strategies. Produce internal communications that build community and credibility, keeping the campus community engaged and informed. Lead the strategy and production of integrated multi-media content for Westminster’s website, social media channels, internal newsletters, enrollment platforms, and university publications.  Develop and execute crisis and emergency communications plans for the university. Develop and plan for weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual reporting on communications campaign performance and impact. Marketing Lead the strategy and production of integrated multi-media content for Westminster’s website, social media channels, internal newsletters, enrollment platforms, and university publications.  Develop and set social media strategy aligned to the university’s goals and strategic communications plans in coordination with the Digital Media Manager and Chief Communications Officer. Serve as a reviewer and proofreader of outbound marketing campaigns and materials to review brand and message alignment. Responsible for analyzing marketing campaign performance to determine effectiveness and make strategic optimizations for future campaigns. Develop and plan for weekly, monthly, quarterly, and annual reporting on marketing campaign performance and impact. Government & Community Outreach In collaboration with the Chief Communications Officer, building relationships with relevant elected representatives at the local, state, and federal level. Leading legislative communications and updates to the campus community. Researching and developing relationships with community organizations, nonprofits, businesses, and other community partners for the university. Organizational & Administrative Tasks Supervise the Marketing Manager and Digital Media Manager, providing regular feedback, guidance, and coaching, along with hiring and training as needed. Other duties as assigned. What we offer: Medical, Dental, and Vision Insurance  Paid Time Off (PTO) Paid Parental Leave Paid Family and Medical Leave 403(b) Retirement Plan with a 5.5% employer contribution Tuition Remission (after a qualifying period of employment) 18 Paid Holidays Annually Cell Phone Plan Discounts Equal Employment Opportunity: Westminster University is committed to a diversified workforce, equal opportunity, and nondiscrimination. To this end, Westminster prohibits any form of discrimination or harassment based on race or ethnicity, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, sexual orientation, gender identification, disabilities, genetic information, status as a veteran, or any other category or classification protected by applicable federal, state, or city laws.",
      "department": ""
    },
